articles.content_en:
Article 1 Where a citizen, legal person, or any other organization considers that any of the following acts relating to the disclosure of government information infringes upon its lawful rights and interests and initiates an administrative action according to law, the People's Court shall accept the case:
(1) applying to an administrative organ for access to government information, and the administrative organ informs the applicant that the government information cannot be provided or will not be processed;
(2) the administrative reconsideration organ makes an administrative reconsideration decision on the refusal to disclose as prescribed in Articles 14, 15, and 16 of the Regulations on the Disclosure of Government Information;
(3) considering that the government information provided by the administrative organ does not conform to the content of its application;
(4) considering that the government information proactively disclosed by the administrative organ or disclosed upon another person's application infringes upon its trade secrets, personal privacy, or other lawful rights and interests;
(5) considering that other acts of the administrative organ in the work of government information disclosure infringe upon its lawful rights and interests.

Article 5 The defendant shall bear the burden of adducing evidence for the legality of its acts such as disclosing or refusing to disclose government information.
Under any of the following circumstances, the defendant shall bear the corresponding burden of adducing evidence:
(1) where the defendant claims that the government information has been disclosed, it shall adduce evidence of the fact of disclosure and submit to the People's Court evidence that it has informed the applicant of the means and channels for obtaining the government information;
(2) where the defendant claims that it decided to disclose government information involving trade secrets or personal privacy due to public interest, it shall adduce evidence of the reasons for determining public interest and that non-disclosure may cause substantial adverse effects on public interest;
(3) where the defendant claims that the information applied for by the plaintiff is internal affairs information and refuses to disclose it, it shall adduce evidence that the information falls within the category of personnel management, logistics management, or internal workflow information;
(4) where the defendant claims that the information applied for by the plaintiff is process information and refuses to disclose it, it shall adduce evidence that the information consists of internal discussion records, draft documents, consultative correspondence, request-for-instructions reports, etc., formed before the administrative organ made the administrative decision;
(5) where the defendant claims that the information applied for by the plaintiff is case file information of administrative law enforcement and refuses to disclose it, it shall adduce evidence that the information consists of party information, investigation records, interrogation transcripts, etc., formed in the process of administrative law enforcement and recorded in the law enforcement case files;
(6) where the defendant claims that the government information does not exist, it shall adduce evidence of the fact that it has performed reasonable search obligations or provide a reasonable explanation.

Article 7 The plaintiff shall bear the burden of adducing evidence for the following matters:
(1) where it initiates an action requesting the defendant to disclose government information, it shall adduce evidence that it previously submitted an application for disclosure of government information to the administrative organ;
(2) where it initiates an action requesting the defendant not to disclose government information, it shall adduce evidence that the government information involves its trade secrets or personal privacy;
(3) adducing evidence that the act of the administrative organ disclosing or refusing to disclose may damage its lawful rights and interests.

Article 10 Where an action concerning government information disclosure initiated by a citizen, legal person, or any other organization obviously fails to meet the conditions for prosecution prescribed by the Administrative Procedure Law, the People's Court shall not register the case for filing.
Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule that the case shall not be filed; if the case has already been filed, the court shall rule to dismiss the prosecution:
(1) the matter is one for which an application shall first be made to an administrative reconsideration organ in accordance with Item 4 of Paragraph 1 of Article 23 of the Administrative Reconsideration Law of the People's Republic of China, but no such application has been made;
(2) the administrative organ makes a procedural notification act such as extending the reply period or requiring the applicant to make corrections;
(3) the action is solely against the administrative organ's decision to charge a fee for processing information;
(4) the applicant repeatedly applies for disclosure of government information that has already been disclosed, and the administrative organ replies that it will not process the duplicate application;
(5) the information the applicant requests the administrative organ to disclose consists of materials such as business registration or real estate registration, and the administrative organ informs the applicant to search for it in accordance with laws or administrative regulations;
(6) the applicant requests the administrative organ to create, process, or analyze government information, and the administrative organ fails to provide it;
(7) the applicant engages in activities such as petitions, complaints, or reports in the form of an application for government information disclosure;
(8) the applicant requests the administrative organ to provide publicly available publications such as government gazettes, newspapers, or books;
(9) considering that a public institution or public enterprise fails to disclose information created or obtained in the course of providing public social services;
(10) other circumstances that do not produce actual effects on the rights and obligations of the citizen, legal person, or any other organization.

Article 11 Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall order the defendant to perform its duty of government information disclosure:
(1) where the defendant refuses to disclose or partially refuses to disclose government information that shall be disclosed according to law, the People's Court shall order the revocation or partial revocation of the contested decision of refusal to disclose, and order the defendant to disclose within twenty working days;
(2) where the defendant fails to reply to the plaintiff's application for disclosure without justifiable grounds within the time limit, and the plaintiff's request for a judgment ordering the defendant to disclose is well-grounded, the People's Court shall order the defendant to disclose within twenty working days;
(3) where the content of the government information that the defendant refuses to disclose can be processed by separation, the People's Court shall order the defendant to disclose the content that can be disclosed within twenty working days;
(4) where the defendant refuses to disclose on the grounds that disclosure of the government information would harm the lawful rights and interests of a third party, but the third party consents to disclosure during the proceedings and the People's Court finds upon trial that disclosure may be made, the People's Court shall order the defendant to disclose within twenty working days.

Article 12 Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall render a judgment confirming the illegality:
(1) the defendant's act of disclosing government information is illegal but the content is not revocable;
(2) the defendant discloses the government information during the proceedings, and the plaintiff still requests confirmation that the original refusal to disclose or failure to reply within the time limit is illegal;
(3) the defendant's act of refusing to disclose or failing to reply is illegal, but a judgment ordering disclosure would be meaningless.

Article 14 Under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall render a judgment dismissing the plaintiff's claims:
(1) the defendant's decision to disclose or refuse to disclose, or its notification that the information cannot be provided or will not be processed, is lawful;
(2) the content of the government information applied for has already been disclosed to the public, and the defendant has informed the applicant of the means, channels, and time for obtaining the government information;
(3) after receiving different applications from the same applicant or applications with identical content from different applicants, the defendant responds in a single government information disclosure reply and the content of the reply is lawful;
(4) the plaintiff's ground for initiating an action against the defendant's failure to reply within the time limit is not established;
(5) the plaintiff's request for non-disclosure on the ground that the government information infringes upon its trade secrets or personal privacy is not established;
(6) other circumstances under which the claims shall be dismissed.
